			title: "The Processors"
			x: -3.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["3584C005DD4926AB"]
			description: [
				"The ME Controller is the heart of a network - it powers and organises everything wired to it. One Controller runs the whole system; a second one placed nearby will conflict with the first."
				""
				"&c&lThis is where the island stops being enough.&r The Controller's corners are no longer sky stone - they are &eDesh Ingots&r, and Desh only exists on the Moon."
				""
				"&7So the road to a real ME network runs: Nether Star, NASA Workbench, rocket, Moon. Everything below this quest you can build at home. Nothing above it.&r"
				""
				"&8You only have to make the trip once. A Desh Bee - a Steel Bee shown a Desh Block - turns the Moon into a renewable supply you can automate."
			]
			icon: "ae2:controller"
			id: "4275E6A1A07E4C54"
			rewards: [{
				id: "56BA37C3A73E3789"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 40
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Desh from the Moon"
			tasks: [{
				id: "2C5A3FCB1805B570"
				item: "ae2:controller"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "The Controller"
			x: -1.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4275E6A1A07E4C54"]
			description: [
				"Networks run on AE energy. An &eEnergy Acceptor&r converts your FE in; an &eEnergy Cell&r stores a buffer."
				""
				"&e&lAE2 uses its own energy unit&r and will not accept FE directly. The Energy Acceptor is the only translator - one on the network, cabled to your grid, and the whole system is powered."
				""
				"&c&lRunning out of power wipes your network state.&r Not your items - those are safe in the cells - but the network shuts down, channels drop, and everything reboots when power returns. Autocrafting jobs in progress are lost."
				""
				"&e&lWhich is why the Energy Cell matters.&r It buffers enough to ride out a brief outage while your generators catch up. On a wind or solar grid that flickers, this is the difference between a stable network and one that resets every night."
				""
				"&7Power draw scales with how much network you have - every bus, every drive, every terminal costs idle power, and autocrafting spikes it hard.&r"
				""
				"&8If your network keeps browning out when a craft starts, add a Dense Energy Cell rather than a bigger generator. The problem is almost always burst demand, not average supply."
			]
			icon: "ae2:energy_acceptor"
			id: "112DBA08A012C0F3"
			rewards: [{
				id: "230C060A445313A7"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7FE in, AE out"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "4157934E8DAC88FE"
					item: "ae2:energy_acceptor"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "1AF842992BC5A1EF"
					item: "ae2:energy_cell"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "Power the Network"
			x: -1.0d
			y: -1.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4275E6A1A07E4C54"]
			description: [
				"An &eME Drive&r holds storage cells. Craft a Drive and a 1k Item Storage Cell - your first digital chest."
				""
				"&e&lStorage cells have two separate limits&r and people usually hit the wrong one first:"
				"&7- &fBytes&7 - total quantity stored"
				"&7- &fTypes&7 - how many &ldistinct&r items it can hold, regardless of quantity"
				""
				"&c&lA 1k cell holds only 63 types.&r Dump a messy inventory into one and it fills on types long before it runs out of bytes - and each additional type also costs bytes. This is why your first cell seems to fill instantly."
				""
				"&e&lPartition your cells.&r Use a &7Cell Workbench&r to lock a cell to specific items. A cell partitioned to just cobblestone stores an enormous amount of it; an unpartitioned cell wastes most of its capacity on 63 kinds of junk."
				""
				"&7A Drive holds 10 cells. Mixing tiers is fine - put your bulk items in a partitioned 1k and your general overflow in a 64k.&r"
				""
				"&8Do not delete cells with items still inside. Break the drive, the cells drop with contents intact - but a cell tossed in lava takes everything with it."
			]
			icon: "ae2:drive"
			id: "5079791596C98418"
			rewards: [{
				id: "68FC27506F9CF480"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 40
			}]
			subtitle: "&7The Drive holds your cells"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "3C16D56B6FA82C24"
					item: "ae2:drive"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "7FE0563C3F752A6F"
					item: "ae2:item_storage_cell_1k"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "First Digital Storage"
			x: 1.0d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5079791596C98418"]
			description: [
				"Scale up: 4k, 16k, and 64k cells hold ever more item types and counts. Fill a Drive with them."
				""
				"&e&lThe type limit barely moves.&r This is the thing that surprises everyone:"
				"&7- &f1k&7 - 63 types"
				"&7- &f4k&7 - 63 types"
				"&7- &f16k&7 - 63 types"
				"&7- &f64k&7 - 63 types"
				""
				"&c&lHigher tiers give you more BYTES, not more TYPES.&r A 64k cell holds vastly more &lquantity&r, but still only 63 distinct items. If your network says it is full while the bytes look fine, you have run out of types and need &lmore cells&r, not bigger ones."
				""
				"&e&lSo build wide, not tall.&r Several mid-tier cells in a Drive beat one enormous cell for a varied inventory. Save the 64k cells for bulk - cobblestone, dust, ore chunks - ideally partitioned."
				""
				"&7This limit is exactly why MEGA Cells exist later, and why they are gated behind Venus. They break the type ceiling as well as the byte one.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:item_storage_cell_4k"
			id: "28AD6C72762CBF0C"
			rewards: [{
				id: "1BE3AE8F3E2B7AC1"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 50
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Cell tiers: 1k to 64k"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "00AAAE25DAAC5B15"
					item: "ae2:item_storage_cell_4k"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "1B2F5B93FA4354EB"
					item: "ae2:item_storage_cell_16k"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "3DBCC1E20D92EB45"
					item: "ae2:item_storage_cell_64k"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "Bigger On The Inside"
			x: 1.0d
			y: -1.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5079791596C98418"]
			description: [
				"A &eCrafting Terminal&r lets you see your whole network and craft from it in one screen."
				""
				"&e&lThe terminal is where you actually live once AE2 is running.&r Search bar at the top, everything you own below it, and a crafting grid attached - pull ingredients straight from the network without opening a single chest."
				""
				"&e&lSearch syntax worth memorising:&r"
				"&7- &f@mod&7 - filter by mod, e.g. `@mekanism`"
				"&7- &f#tag&7 - filter by tag, e.g. `#ingot`"
				"&7- &f*&7 - wildcard"
				"&7- Prefix with &f-&7 to exclude"
				""
				"&e&lTerminal variants&r do different jobs: the plain &7ME Terminal&r views only, the &7Crafting Terminal&r adds the grid, and the &7Pattern Encoding Terminal&r writes autocrafting patterns. Later, AE2WTLib's Universal Terminal merges all of them into one item."
				""
				"&7Terminals are cable parts, not blocks - attach them to the side of a cable and they cost a channel each.&r"
				""
				"&8Set the terminal to sort by amount and enable the tooltip showing item counts. Small settings, big difference when your network holds thousands of things."
			]
			icon: "ae2:crafting_terminal"
			id: "730CBB4200699C94"
			rewards: [{
				id: "6675CEEDD228F0BA"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 40
			}]
			subtitle: "&7See and search everything"
			tasks: [{
				id: "501CD9E39BB5D449"
				item: "ae2:crafting_terminal"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "The Terminal"
			x: 3.0d
			y: 0.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["730CBB4200699C94"]
			description: [
				"AUTOCRAFTING. A Pattern Provider holds recipes; a Molecular Assembler builds them. Encode a pattern, ask the terminal for the result, and the network assembles it on demand."
				""
				"This is the moment AE2 stops being storage and becomes a factory."
				""
				"&b&lIt costs you the Moon.&r The Assembler's iron frame is now &eDesh&r - and Desh does not exist on your island, in the Nether, or anywhere the sieve can reach. It generates on the &eMoon&r and nowhere else."
				""
				"&7Storage, terminals and import/export all still work without it. You can run a perfectly good network while you build the rocket - you just cannot make it craft for you.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:molecular_assembler"
			id: "67906B4F774BA557"
			rewards: [{
				id: "7FD4FEEC0B1D8A5D"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 60
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Build the rocket first"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "3DA38849DECF8273"
					item: "ae2:pattern_provider"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "77120B9EE40A447F"
					item: "ae2:molecular_assembler"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "Autocrafting Begins"
			x: 5.0d
			y: 0.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["67906B4F774BA557"]
			description: [
				"A Crafting CPU is a multiblock of Crafting Units and Storage. Storage sets how many distinct items a job can hold; Accelerators set how fast it chews through them. Build several small CPUs rather than one huge one - the network can only run one job per CPU."
				""
				"Build a 1k Storage, a Unit and an Accelerator to form your first CPU."
				""
				"&b&lAlso Desh-gated.&r Crafting Units share the Assembler's tax, so the whole autocrafting tree lands on the far side of your first rocket. Budget the Moon trip for a stack of Desh, not four ingots - you will want several CPUs eventually."
			]
			icon: "ae2:crafting_unit"
			id: "7A7F425862B38C87"
			rewards: [{
				id: "6C7C6C6F2E29D6F9"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 60
			}]
			subtitle: "&7One job per CPU - build several"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "123A0AA20CBAE381"
					item: "ae2:crafting_unit"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "6A31A8F583B38772"
					item: "ae2:1k_crafting_storage"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "41D0D8D8D521EB73"
					item: "ae2:crafting_accelerator"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "Build a Crafting CPU"
			x: 7.0d
			y: 0.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["67906B4F774BA557"]
			description: [
				"The &ePattern Encoding Terminal&r is where you write autocrafting patterns. Encode a few and watch the network build them."
				""
				"&e&lTwo pattern types, and picking the wrong one is the usual reason autocrafting fails:&r"
				"&7- &fCrafting Pattern&7 - a 3x3 grid recipe. Executed by a &fMolecular Assembler&7."
				"&7- &fProcessing Pattern&7 - arbitrary inputs and outputs, for machines. The network pushes ingredients into the machine and waits for the result to come back."
				""
				"&c&lProcessing patterns do not care about recipe shape&r - you tell it what goes in and what comes out. Use these for anything that is not a crafting table: smelters, crushers, infusers, the lot."
				""
				"&e&lPatterns live in a Pattern Provider&r, and the Provider must be touching the machine (or an assembler) that executes them. A pattern sitting in a Provider with nothing attached is a recipe the network believes it can make and then silently stalls on."
				""
				"&e&lSubstitutions&r let a pattern accept any equivalent ingredient - any wood plank, any iron source. Toggle it per-slot in the terminal. Fuzzy matching helps for damaged tools."
				""
				"&7Encode everything. A pattern costs one blank pattern and takes ten seconds; the payoff is never crafting that item by hand again.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:pattern_encoding_terminal"
			id: "30E15333874AE5C1"
			rewards: [{
				id: "5CDC6382731BCD06"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 40
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Teach the network a recipe"
			tasks: [{
				id: "78BCB14741BDBF21"
				item: "ae2:pattern_encoding_terminal"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "Encode Patterns"
			x: 5.0d
			y: 2.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["7A7F425862B38C87"]
			description: [
				"&eImport&r and &eExport Buses&r pull from and push to machines automatically - the veins of a self-running factory."
				""
				"&e&lThink of them from the network's point of view:&r"
				"&7- &fImport Bus&7 - takes items &lout of&r a machine and &linto&r the network"
				"&7- &fExport Bus&7 - takes items &lout of&r the network and &linto&r a machine"
				""
				"&c&lExport Buses need a filter&r or they do nothing at all. Import Buses default to taking everything, which is usually what you want."
				""
				"&e&lUpgrade cards change their behaviour:&r"
				"&7- &fSpeed Card&7 - more items per operation, up to 4"
				"&7- &fCapacity Card&7 - more filter slots"
				"&7- &fRedstone Card&7 - only run on a redstone signal"
				"&7- &fCrafting Card&7 - if the network has none of the item, &lrequest a craft&r instead of idling. This is what makes auto-stocking work."
				"&7- &fFuzzy Card&7 - ignore damage and NBT when matching"
				""
				"&e&lThe classic setup:&r Export Bus with a Crafting Card feeding a machine, Import Bus taking the output back. That loop is a self-refilling production line and it is the point of the whole mod."
				""
				"&8Every bus costs a channel. Watch your controller's budget as you add them."
			]
			icon: "ae2:import_bus"
			id: "028C42F38D7735BD"
			rewards: [{
				id: "50CCA33C0CF8F872"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 40
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Import and export buses"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "4E210511981FCF17"
					item: "ae2:import_bus"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "5FEC1DD4B8302C10"
					item: "ae2:export_bus"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "Full Automation"
			x: 9.0d
			y: 0.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["730CBB4200699C94"]
			description: [
				"A &eWireless Terminal&r + &eAccess Point&r lets you reach your whole network from anywhere. No more running home."
				""
				"&e&lYou need both halves.&r The terminal is the handheld item; the &7Wireless Access Point&r is what it talks to. A terminal with no access point in range simply does nothing."
				""
				"&e&lRange is the thing to plan around.&r A bare Access Point has a small radius. Slot &7Wireless Booster&r cards into it and the range grows substantially, and you can place several access points to cover a sprawling base."
				""
				"&c&lIt does not cross dimensions.&r Standing on the Moon, your island's access point is unreachable. That is what the &7Quantum Ring&r is for - link the two networks, then put an access point on each end."
				""
				"&e&lThe terminal holds a charge&r and drains with use. Keep it topped up in a charger."
				""
				"&7AE2WTLib upgrades this into a Universal Terminal - crafting, patterns and storage in one handheld item. Worth it the moment you can afford it.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:wireless_terminal"
			id: "3343C89DAB433A2D"
			rewards: [{
				id: "703761F4660E6E1E"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 40
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Leave the terminal behind"
			tasks: [{
				id: "514C9F3EA6DFF0E4"
				item: "ae2:wireless_terminal"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "Go Wireless"
			x: 3.0d
			y: 2.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["7A7F425862B38C87"]
			description: [
				"Spatial Storage captures an entire region of the world into a cell - move whole builds, or pocket-dimension your base."
				""
				"Build Spatial Pylons and a Spatial IO Port."
			]
			icon: "ae2:spatial_pylon"
