		}
		{
			dependencies: ["1FA9577AA6C0D787"]
			description: [
				"&eGrowth Accelerators&r placed beside budding certus quartz make it grow dramatically faster."
				""
				"&e&lThis is the sustainable certus supply&r, and it is the difference between AE2 being viable and being a grind. Budding quartz blocks sprout crystals over time; accelerators speed that up; you harvest and the block regrows."
				""
				"&c&lBudding blocks degrade as you harvest&r - Flawless to Flawed to Chipped to Damaged, then plain quartz. Mine them with a Fortune tool or, better, use the in-world fluix growth loop for bulk."
				""
				"&7Accelerators need power. A small array with four accelerators around one budding block produces more certus than early AE2 can consume.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:growth_accelerator"
			id: "5714B172FC3D8D9E"
			rewards: [{
				id: "69BC6A7D699C552C"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Certus that farms itself"
			tasks: [{
				id: "6F5059C7764055BF"
				item: "ae2:growth_accelerator"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bCrystal Growth"
			x: -9.0d
			y: -2.5d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"AE2 has four cable types and they are not cosmetic."
				""
				"&7- &fGlass&7 - cheapest, carries 8 channels."
				"&7- &fCovered&7 - same 8 channels, does not visually connect to everything."
				"&7- &fSmart&7 - 8 channels, and &lshows channel usage as coloured lines&r. Worth the cost for that alone."
				"&7- &fDense (smart or covered)&7 - carries &l32 channels&r."
				""
				"&e&lChannels are AE2's core constraint.&r Every device - drive, terminal, bus, interface - consumes one. A controller supplies a limited number, and a plain cable run can only carry 8 to any branch."
				""
				"&c&lRun DENSE cable from the controller as a trunk&r, then split to normal cable near the devices. Trying to feed thirty devices down one glass cable is the classic beginner network failure."
				""
				"&7Smart cable's coloured lines tell you at a glance which branch is saturated. Use it while building, even if you swap it later.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:fluix_smart_cable"
			id: "4A2562FC80C2A537"
			rewards: [{
				id: "2DB1B94CFCE53776"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Glass, covered, smart, dense"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "27451607AD4D75A2"
					item: "ae2:fluix_glass_cable"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "46DE7F76A182DE16"
					item: "ae2:fluix_smart_cable"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "&bCable Types"
			x: -9.0d
			y: -4.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"A &eP2P Tunnel&r pair carries something from one point to another through your existing cable - items, fluids, energy, redstone, light, or ME channels themselves."
				""
				"&e&lThe ME variant is the important one.&r A dense cable carries 32 channels; a P2P tunnel pair lets you push those 32 channels down a single cable run to a remote sub-network. That is how large networks stay manageable."
				""
				"&e&lHow to pair them:&r right-click the input tunnel with a &7Memory Card&r to copy its frequency, then right-click each output tunnel to paste it. One input, many outputs."
				""
				"&7Non-ME tunnels are just as useful - run FE, fluids and items through the same cable you already laid instead of three parallel pipe runs.&r"
				""
				"&8This is the block that separates a tidy AE2 base from a spaghetti one."
			]
			icon: "ae2:me_p2p_tunnel"
			id: "5D8B26978072FC06"
			rewards: [{
				id: "66F7DB3B09AE8B49"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7One cable, many services"
			tasks: [{
				id: "157EF30FF106D21C"
				item: "ae2:me_p2p_tunnel"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bP2P Tunnels"
			x: -9.0d
			y: -5.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5079791596C98418"]
			description: [
				"A &eStorage Bus&r on a chest, barrel, drawer or machine makes its contents appear in your ME terminal as if they were in a cell."
				""
				"&e&lThis is how you keep bulk storage cheap.&r Cobblestone, gravel, dust and ore chunks do not belong in expensive cells - put them in Storage Drawers and slap a storage bus on the controller. The network sees them, you never pay for cell types."
				""
				"&e&lPriority matters.&r Set the bus's priority higher than your cells and the network will prefer to put matching items there. That is how you route specific items to specific containers automatically."
				""
				"&7A storage bus with a filter only accepts what you list, which turns any chest into a targeted overflow.&r"
				""
				"&8Also works on other mods' storage - a bus on a Mekanism QIO or a Refined Storage network bridges the two."
			]
			icon: "ae2:storage_bus"
			id: "2C838F63119A5CA9"
			rewards: [{
				id: "29F73684676F0F42"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Make any chest part of the network"
			tasks: [{
				id: "2D39D69E2D42E87A"
				item: "ae2:storage_bus"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bStorage Bus"
			x: -7.0d
			y: 2.5d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"An &eME Interface&r exposes network items to a machine or pipe; a &ePattern Provider&r pushes crafting ingredients into machines."
				""
				"&e&lInterfaces can also stock themselves.&r Set an item and a quantity, and the interface keeps that much on hand, crafting more if needed. That is how you keep a machine permanently fed without an export bus."
				""
				"&e&lPattern Providers are the autocrafting output.&r They hold your encoded patterns and push ingredients to whatever they touch - a Molecular Assembler for crafting recipes, or any machine for processing patterns."
				""
				"&c&lA Pattern Provider must physically touch the machine&r, or be pointed at it with a cable-facing variant. A provider with nothing adjacent will accept a craft job and then stall it forever."
				""
				"&7Extended AE's Expanded Pattern Provider holds 72 patterns instead of 9 - a straight upgrade once you have a lot of recipes.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:interface"
			id: "652EF2A6E105C1B1"
			rewards: [{
				id: "4B668098181064D8"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7The network's hands"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "3E29C6EBA7392AE3"
					item: "ae2:interface"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "0C5C569206646EC5"
					item: "ae2:pattern_provider"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "&bInterface & Pattern Provider"
			x: -7.0d
			y: 4.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"A &eLevel Emitter&r emits a redstone signal when a chosen item in the network rises above or falls below a threshold you set."
				""
				"&e&lThis is the network's feedback loop.&r Emitter watching your steel count, wired to the machine that makes steel - production stops at a cap and restarts when you spend it."
				""
				"&7It can also watch crafting status rather than stock, emitting while a craft is in progress.&r"
				""
				"&8Combine with a Toggle Bus to switch whole sections of network on and off - useful for keeping idle power draw down on a big base."
			]
			icon: "ae2:level_emitter"
			id: "044623BF57F787F6"
			rewards: [{
				id: "0F97DFB71B90126B"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Redstone from stock levels"
			tasks: [{
				id: "74542AAD71E37E9F"
				item: "ae2:level_emitter"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bLevel Emitter"
			x: -5.0d
			y: 4.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"An &eAnnihilation Plane&r breaks the block in front of it and stores the drop; a &eFormation Plane&r places blocks from the network into the world."
				""
				"&e&lThe pair is a complete automated production line with no machines.&r Formation plane places a block, annihilation plane breaks it, network keeps the difference - that is how cobble generators, obsidian farms and tree farms get built in AE2."
				""
				"&7Annihilation planes also pick up items and XP that touch them, which makes them the tidiest mob-farm collector in the pack.&r"
				""
				"&8Fit them with speed and fortune cards. A fortune-enchanted annihilation plane on an ore block is a genuinely good multiplier."
			]
			icon: "ae2:annihilation_plane"
			id: "1E2150C2C3C4C452"
			rewards: [{
				id: "188E59C4A9686433"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7The network touches the world"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "7C7A478097CFB0B6"
					item: "ae2:annihilation_plane"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "790FE57ABCE71289"
					item: "ae2:formation_plane"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "&bFormation & Annihilation Planes"
			x: -5.0d
			y: 5.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5079791596C98418"]
			description: [
				"The &eCell Workbench&r configures a storage cell - which items it accepts, and in what order the network fills it."
				""
				"&c&lThis is the fix for the 63-type limit.&r An unpartitioned cell wastes most of its capacity holding one stack each of sixty-three kinds of junk. Partition it to a handful of items and it holds an enormous amount of each."
				""
				"&e&lThe standard setup:&r one 64k cell partitioned to your bulk materials, several unpartitioned mid-tier cells for everything else, and storage buses on drawers for the truly high-volume stuff."
				""
				"&7The workbench also copies a configuration between cells, so you can set one up and clone it.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:cell_workbench"
			id: "1F7659A11AFEC31E"
			rewards: [{
				id: "488D655902EFE9BA"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Partition your storage"
			tasks: [{
				id: "224B9BC4E1B63DB7"
				item: "ae2:cell_workbench"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bCell Workbench"
			x: -5.0d
			y: 2.5d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"&eFluid Storage Cells&r hold liquids in the same drives as your item cells, viewable and extractable from the terminal."
				""
				"&e&lThis matters more in this pack than most&r, because Mekanism runs on gases and fluids that otherwise need their own tank farms. An ME fluid cell replaces a wall of tanks and puts everything on one screen."
				""
				"&7Applied Mekanistics extends AE2 to Mekanism chemicals specifically, so oxygen, hydrogen and the rest go in the same system.&r"
				""
				"&8Fluid cells have the same type limits as item cells. Partition them the same way."
			]
			icon: "ae2:fluid_storage_cell_1k"
			id: "1793251D802CE994"
			rewards: [{
				id: "53380AB88B22B82C"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Store more than items"
			tasks: [{
				id: "39983BAAE48EB691"
				item: "ae2:fluid_storage_cell_1k"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bFluid & Chemical Cells"
			x: -3.0d
			y: 2.5d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"An &eME IO Port&r bulk-transfers cells in or out of the network; a &eMatter Condenser&r destroys items, optionally turning them into singularities."
				""
				"&e&lThe IO Port is how you empty a cell&r without pulling items one stack at a time - drop cells in, hit transfer, and the network absorbs everything."
				""
				"&e&lThe Condenser is your void bin.&r Point a storage bus at it with a filter and the network silently deletes overflow cobblestone. Set to Singularity mode it instead consumes enormous quantities of matter to produce the singularities that Quantum Bridges need."
				""
				"&c&lThe Condenser destroys permanently.&r Filter it carefully - a mis-set storage bus on a condenser will eat your entire network."
			]
			icon: "ae2:io_port"
			id: "6533A593E80924EB"
			rewards: [{
				id: "1D16C7D7AA02B287"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Bulk cell management, and a bin"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "65770D507EEE9973"
					item: "ae2:io_port"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "298491823B8C52F9"
					item: "ae2:condenser"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "&bIO Port & Condenser"
			x: -3.0d
			y: 4.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"A &eStorage Monitor&r displays how much of one item the network holds; a &eConversion Monitor&r does the same and lets you deposit or withdraw by clicking it."
				""
				"&e&lA wall of these is a dashboard&r - key materials at a glance without opening a terminal. Lock one with the network tool and it stays on that item."
				""
				"&7Conversion monitors are genuinely convenient at a build site: click with a stack to deposit, click empty-handed to withdraw one, crouch-click for a full stack.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:storage_monitor"
			id: "5ED6ADA1949CC240"
			rewards: [{
				id: "73E59F94EC632ED7"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Read and touch stock"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "1815547E03786EC5"
					item: "ae2:storage_monitor"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "71AD9CF9CC9783A9"
					item: "ae2:conversion_monitor"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "&bStorage & Conversion Monitors"
			x: -1.0d
			y: 4.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"The &eME Security Terminal&r controls who can access your network and what they can do."
				""
				"&e&lOn a shared server this is worth doing early.&r Without it, anyone who reaches a terminal has your entire storage. Set per-player permissions for build, extract, inject, craft and security."
				""
				"&7It also produces linked Wireless Terminals - a terminal registered to a secured network only works for players you have authorised.&r"
				""
				"&8Only one security terminal per network."
			]
			icon: "ae2:security_station"
			id: "69C3FB8C2BF4A6DD"
			rewards: [{
				id: "067EBF52FBD51334"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Lock the network"
			tasks: [{
				id: "1D6E0EAEF8664738"
				item: "ae2:security_station"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bSecurity Terminal"
			x: 1.0d
			y: 4.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"The &eNetwork Tool&r shows a network's full device list and lets you install upgrade cards without breaking anything; the &eColor Applicator&r recolours cables."
				""
				"&e&lThe network tool is a diagnostic.&r Hold it and right-click any cable to see every device, its channel use and its status. That is how you find the branch that is out of channels."
				""
				"&e&lColour is functional, not cosmetic.&r Cables only connect to cables of the same colour or to fluix-coloured ones, so recolouring is how you run two independent networks through the same wall."
				""
				"&7AE2 also has an Entropy Manipulator (smelt or freeze blocks in place), a Matter Cannon (fires stored matter) and a Charged Staff. All powered from the network.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:network_tool"
			id: "33DAE58708C2B807"
			rewards: [{
				id: "352817BCC60485D7"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Quality of life"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "776371FEF79CC7B5"
					item: "ae2:network_tool"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "4AB7B80B58FE3CB5"
					item: "ae2:color_applicator"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "&bNetwork Tools & Gear"
			x: 1.0d
			y: 5.5d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"An &eME Chest&r holds a single storage cell and gives you a terminal on the front. No controller, no cables, no channels."
				""
				"&e&lThis is the cheapest possible entry to AE2&r and a genuinely good early-game chest - one cell holds far more than a double chest and it is searchable."
				""
				"&7It is also the correct thing to take to a new build site or another dimension. Drop a chest, drop a cell, and you have organised storage anywhere.&r"
				""
				"&8Feed it power with an energy acceptor or it will not open."
			]
			icon: "ae2:chest"
			id: "1909EB9D3CC75812"
			rewards: [{
				id: "5367EA823DE44A60"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7A network of one"
			tasks: [{
				id: "0F3B6443C3A92ABA"
				item: "ae2:chest"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bME Chest"
			x: -1.0d
			y: 2.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["67906B4F774BA557"]
			description: [
				"&eCrafting Co-Processing Units&r - accelerators - let a Crafting CPU work on more steps of a job in parallel."
				""
				"&e&lA CPU with no accelerators crafts one step at a time&r, which on a deep recipe tree is agonising. Each accelerator adds another parallel worker."
				""
				"&e&lCPU design, briefly:&r &7Storage&r sets how many distinct items the job can hold, &7Accelerators&r set speed, and a &7Crafting Monitor&r shows what it is doing. Build several small CPUs rather than one giant one - the network runs one job per CPU, so three CPUs means three simultaneous crafts."
				""
				"&7MEGA Cells adds a MEGA Crafting Accelerator that is worth the Venus trip if you are doing serious autocrafting.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:crafting_accelerator"
			id: "7FAD9215CF11B4A6"
			rewards: [{
				id: "34F3B12A3715A4DE"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Make jobs finish"
			tasks: [{
				id: "787B1577052F98CE"
				item: "ae2:crafting_accelerator"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bCrafting Accelerators"
			x: 5.0d
			y: -2.5d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"A &eCrafting Monitor&r attached to a CPU displays the job currently running on it."
				""
				"&e&lThis is how you diagnose a stalled craft.&r A job that sits forever is almost always missing an ingredient with no pattern, or a Pattern Provider with nothing adjacent to push into. The monitor names the item it is waiting on."
				""
				"&7Name your CPUs with an anvil-renamed Crafting Unit so the monitor tells you which one is busy.&r"
			]
			icon: "ae2:crafting_monitor"
			id: "7672FC5AE642EFD0"
			rewards: [{
				id: "414107D05F93792C"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7See what the network is doing"
			tasks: [{
				id: "1A163A1A538C3DF4"
				item: "ae2:crafting_monitor"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&bCrafting Monitor"
			x: 7.0d
			y: -2.5d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"A &eToggle Bus&r cuts a section of network on a redstone signal; a &eQuartz Fiber&r passes power between two networks without passing channels."
				""
				"&e&lQuartz fiber is the trick for sub-networks.&r A storage-bus-only sub-network attached by fiber draws power from your main network but keeps its own channel budget - which is how you attach dozens of storage buses without exhausting the controller."
				""
				"&7Sub-networks are the advanced AE2 pattern. Once you understand fiber, channel limits stop being a constraint.&r"
				""
				"&8Toggle buses paired with level emitters let the network switch parts of itself off when idle."
			]
			icon: "ae2:toggle_bus"
			id: "667F2847FF4FF7B8"
			rewards: [{
				id: "6A0419973848A19D"
				type: "xp"
				xp: 30
			}]
			subtitle: "&7Split and switch"
			tasks: [
				{
					id: "4AE28AD521B9E68E"
					item: "ae2:toggle_bus"
					type: "item"
				}
				{
					id: "40D18BDEAACA3BB5"
					item: "ae2:quartz_fiber"
					type: "item"
				}
			]
			title: "&bToggle Bus & Quartz Fiber"
			x: 3.0d
			y: 4.0d
		}
		{
			description: [
				"&b&lYou understand the network.&r"
				""
				"Dense trunks and P2P tunnels instead of cable spaghetti. Storage buses on drawers instead of expensive cells. Sub-networks behind quartz fibre so channels stopped mattering. Several small CPUs so three jobs run at once."
				""
				"&7AE2 is the mod in this pack with the highest skill ceiling and the harshest early learning curve. Everything above this quest is what separates a network that works from one that scales.&r"
				""
				"&8What is left is MEGA Cells and Spatial Storage - both behind Venus."
			]
			icon: "ae2:controller"
			id: "36188A1AB4313FC1"
